-- #1427 (alias-importer re-export of a CONSTRAINED binding) -- THE POSITIVE ROW
-- REPLACING THE DRAINED PIN `1427-alias-reexport-constrained-build-fails`. A pin
-- asserts a bug STILL REPRODUCES, so a fix deletes it and the S1 has NO guard
-- unless a positive row replaces it.
--
-- SAME conjunction as the unconstrained sibling (`export import` re-export + an
-- ALIAS importer spelling) with ONE dimension flipped: the callee is CONSTRAINED
-- (`Num a => a -> a`), so the call site needs a dictionary and the failure is
-- observable in the VALUE rather than only in the symbol table.
--
-- GROUND TRUTH, HAND-DERIVED FROM THE DECLARATIONS BEFORE ANY BINARY WAS RUN:
--   `f : Num a => a -> a`, `f x = x + 1`. `R.f 1` instantiates at `Int`
--   (defaulting the `Num` goal to the literal's type), so `R.f 1` = 2 and every
--   verb prints `2`.
--
-- ALL FOUR CELLS ARE NOW ACCEPT (#2840). Two repairs, on two channels, landed
-- one after the other and only the pair makes the row green:
--   * the BUILD arm was repaired by the scheme-environment fix (#1671), which is
--     definer-keyed rather than spelling-keyed; pre-fix `medaka build` failed
--     outright, exit 1, `E-PANIC: unbound constrained fn: lib_decl__f`, no binary.
--   * the RUN arm was repaired by #2840. `medaka run` panicked
--     `E-PANIC: intToString: not an Int` (exit 1, EMPTY stdout) because
--     `aliasDictNamesOfPath`'s alias arm asked `declTopFnNames` what the SPELLED
--     module defines, and a pure re-exporter defines nothing -- so `R.f` never
--     joined the dict-name set, the call site was never marked as a dict
--     application, and the callee ran with the dictionary sitting where its `Int`
--     argument belonged. That arm now also resolves through `graphPubDefiners`,
--     the same identity-off-the-graph route `aliasEntriesFor` already took for the
--     dict-ARITY table.
--
-- Earlier revisions of this header attributed the run residue to
-- `aliasEntriesFor`/`aliasConstraintEntries` and pinned the `run` cell as
-- REJECT-as-measured. That attribution was instrumented and REFUTED: on the
-- pre-fix binary `graphPubDefiners "lib.rsel"` already answered `f <- lib.decl.f`
-- and the arity table was correctly re-keyed; what came back EMPTY was
-- `aliasDictNames`, one seam further on.
--
-- TWO INDEPENDENT WITNESSES live beside this fixture rather than inside it, so a
-- repair that happened to suit depth 1 at `Int` cannot pass for a general one:
--   `s8-xmod-reexport-alias-constrained-depth3`       -- a depth-3 chain at `Int`
--   `s8-xmod-reexport-alias-constrained-depth3-float` -- the same at `Float`
-- The `Float` cell is the one that proves the dict is the RIGHT one: a
-- wrongly-routed `Num` dictionary cannot print `5.0`.
import lib.rsel as R

main = println (R.f 1)
